#N canvas 0 50 820 2006 12;
#X text 20 14 How a patch hears and sends - a process cue in Go.dot, f 96;
#X text 20 44 A process cue's patch runs inside Go.dot while its cue runs - not in this window. Here you write it: save (Ctrl+S) and Go.dot takes the patch \, and a running cue starts it again. So nothing arrives in this window \, and nothing sent here leaves it. A patch names what it hears and where it sends by address - there is nothing to set up beside it., f 96;
#X text 20 128 OSC FROM A DEVICE, f 96;
#X text 20 158 Add the device in Show settings > Network: where it is \, the start of its addresses (its prefix) \, and Rx on. It sends to Go.dot's OSC port - 8010 unless the show says another - and Go.dot hands each value to the patches listening for its address., f 96;
#X obj 20 212 r /wfs/source/1/x;
#X obj 20 252 print x;
#X text 260 212 the last value the device reported at that address \, the tick it came in: a number \, a word or a list, f 60;
#X floatatom 20 292 6 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 20 322 s /wfs/source/1/y;
#X text 260 292 writes to the device \, as an OSC cue aimed there would, f 60;
#X text 20 376 GO.DOT ITSELF, f 96;
#X msg 20 406 \; /godot/cmd/cue/fire 3.1;
#X text 300 406 fires cue 3.1 by its number \, as a button on a surface would - in the list in front first \, then the others. No cue with that number and it is refused, f 56;
#X msg 20 466 \; /godot/cmd/cue/fire B3NK0001;
#X text 300 466 fires a cue by its id \, which a renumbering does not break. Right-click the cue in the list \, Copy for a patch - or drag the cue onto Go.dot's canvas, f 56;
#X msg 20 526 \; /godot/cmd/go;
#X text 300 526 GO. Any named command is /godot/cmd/ and its name: standby/set \, run/stopAll... Enable \, Disable and Jump to are transport cues \, fired by number or id, f 56;
#X obj 20 586 r /godot/dca/K2M9X4QA/trim;
#X text 300 586 a row of the show or of the engine - here a DCA's trim - each time it changes. Go.dot's OSC console \, the page in a browser \, lists every address, f 56;
#X floatatom 20 646 6 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 20 676 s /godot/dca/K2M9X4QA/trim;
#X text 300 646 sets a row \, as the window would - refused while the show is locked. In a command's words \, self is this cue or its run: cue/fire self fires this cue, f 56;
#X text 20 730 MIDI, f 96;
#X text 20 760 Pick the ports in the cue's inspector: MIDI in \, MIDI out. Pd's own MIDI settings play no part., f 96;
#X obj 20 790 notein;
#X obj 132 790 ctlin;
#X obj 244 790 pgmin;
#X obj 356 790 bendin;
#X obj 468 790 touchin;
#X obj 580 790 polytouchin;
#X obj 692 790 midiin;
#X obj 804 790 sysexin;
#X text 20 824 note \, controller \, program \, bend \, aftertouch \, poly aftertouch - [midiin] every byte \, [sysexin] system exclusive, f 96;
#X obj 20 864 noteout;
#X obj 145 864 ctlout;
#X obj 270 864 pgmout;
#X obj 395 864 bendout;
#X obj 520 864 touchout;
#X obj 645 864 polytouchout;
#X obj 770 864 midiout;
#X text 20 922 SERIAL: AN ARDUINO AND THE LIKE, f 96;
#X text 20 952 Add the port in Show settings > Serial. Right-click its row \, Copy for a patch \, for these two boxes with its id., f 96;
#X obj 20 986 r /godot/serial/K7Q2M9X4/in;
#X obj 20 1026 unpack f f;
#X text 300 986 each line it sends \, split on spaces and commas \, numbers as numbers - every line \, not only a changed one, f 56;
#X msg 20 1066 led 1;
#X obj 20 1096 s /godot/serial/K7Q2M9X4/out;
#X text 300 1066 a line to the port \, its words joined by spaces, f 56;
#X text 20 1150 MQTT: A SENSOR \, A CONTROLLER OR A HUB ON A BROKER, f 96;
#X text 20 1180 Add the device in Show settings > Network: its address cell offers "On an MQTT broker \, at an address..." or "On Go.dot's MQTT broker" (the switch under the table) \, and its topics from the same menu - # for everything. Give it a prefix \, /house say: a topic is then an address under it \, room1/temp is /house/room1/temp., f 96;
#X obj 20 1252 r /house/room1/temp;
#X text 300 1252 what was published on the topic \, its payload read as a serial line is: numbers as numbers \, words as words, f 56;
#X msg 20 1302 1;
#X obj 20 1332 s /house/lights/1;
#X text 300 1302 publishes 1 on lights/1 through the device's broker, f 56;
#X text 20 1386 THE SPACEMOUSE, f 96;
#X obj 20 1416 r /godot/puck;
#X obj 20 1456 unpack f f f f f f;
#X text 300 1416 its six axes \, each tick \, while the patch listens: tx ty tz rx ry rz \, each -1 to 1, f 56;
#X text 20 1510 ANY ADDRESS, f 96;
#X obj 20 1540 r in;
#X obj 20 1580 route /wfs/source/1/x;
#X text 300 1540 everything this patch could hear \, as an address and its values - for names a patch builds as it runs, f 56;
#X msg 20 1620 /wfs/source/2/x 0.5;
#X obj 20 1650 s out;
#X text 300 1620 any of the sends above \, the address first, f 56;
#X text 20 1704 WHAT A PATCH SAYS, f 96;
#X obj 20 1734 print;
#X text 300 1734 its last line shows in the cue's running pane \, and on the canvas while the cue runs, f 56;
#X text 20 1788 NOT THIS WAY, f 96;
#X text 20 1818 [netreceive] \, [netsend] and [oscparse] on a port of the patch's own: Go.dot already listens \, the port may be Go.dot's \, and what comes in that way is not in the show's log \, so a replay cannot play it back. Add the device instead., f 96;
#X text 20 1886 READY-MADE, f 96;
#X text 20 1916 go.avg \, go.minmax \, go.smooth \, go.scale \, go.deadband \, go.change \, go.edge \, go.hold \, go.ratelimit - right-click one in a patch and choose Help., f 96;
